course: dictionary
lesson: 14671
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The clues in the sentence show you which meaning is right. Read the words around the word, then ask: which meaning would make sense here?
explain: «Our family pays a monthly premium for car insurance.» Monthly and insurance tell you that premium is money paid regularly, to keep your cover safe.
explain: «She bought premium ice cream, which cost a little more but tasted richer.» Here premium means high quality. Try your meaning in the sentence: «She bought high-quality ice cream» still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Premium can be a noun or an adjective.
fact: An insurance premium is the money you pay regularly to keep your insurance.
fact: «Premium» as an adjective means high quality and usually more expensive, as in «premium petrol».
fact: «At a premium» means hard to find, so it is in great demand: «Space is at a premium in our flat.»
fact: «Put a premium on» something means you treat it as very important.
exercise: «Our family pays a monthly premium for car insurance.» What is a premium here? | A map of the roads | A prize for winning a race | A type of car | *Money paid regularly for insurance
exercise: «She bought premium ice cream, which cost a little more but tasted richer.» What does premium suggest? | Cheap and watery | *High quality, and usually more expensive | Free to take home | Cold and frozen
exercise: «Space is at a premium in our flat.» What does at a premium mean? | *Hard to find and in great demand | Free for everyone | Painted in bright colours | Very large and empty
test: «Dad says the shop's premium petrol is worth the extra cost.» What does premium mean here? | *Of high quality and usually more expensive | Very slow to arrive | Free of charge | Hidden under the counter
test: «Tickets for the concert were at a premium, so we bought ours early.» What does at a premium tell you? | *They were hard to get and in great demand | They were lost in the post | They were sold in the morning only | They were given away for free
exam: «Mum said, 'I put a premium on honesty, so always tell me the truth.'» What does put a premium on mean? | Ignore something that is not important | *Treat something as very important | Forget about something | Pay extra money for honesty
